# Mobile Applications (Native + Cross-platform)

**Intent:** Build high-quality mobile apps with platform-correct UX, predictable state, and production-ready release gates.

## Scope

- iOS (`SwiftUI`)
- Android (`Jetpack Compose`)
- Cross-platform (`React Native`, `Flutter`)
- Mobile-first PWAs that behave like app shells

## Build order

1. Define one primary job-to-be-done per screen.
2. Sketch navigation model (tabs, stack, modal, deep links).
3. Define semantic design tokens (color, type, spacing, radius, elevation).
4. Build core screens with accessibility + loading/error/empty states.
5. Add analytics, crash reporting, and release checks.

## UI system baseline

- Keep one source of truth for tokens.
- Prefer semantic tokens over hardcoded values.
- Keep touch targets >=44pt iOS / >=48dp Android.
- Do not rely on color only for status.
- Respect reduced motion and dynamic type / font scaling.

## Platform behavior rules

- Follow native patterns before custom patterns.
- iOS: prioritize tab + stack clarity, swipe-back compatibility.
- Android: preserve system back and predictable top app bar behavior.
- Cross-platform: match platform conventions where users expect them.

## Reliability and state

- Define explicit offline, loading, error, and success states.
- Persist user-critical drafts locally when flows are multi-step.
- Prevent duplicate submits with disabled/loading button states.
- Use optimistic updates only when rollback is defined.

## Performance gate

- Keep startup lightweight; lazy-load heavy features.
- Virtualize long lists.
- Avoid layout thrash and unnecessary rerenders/rebuilds.
- Compress and size media assets for mobile network constraints.

## Security and privacy

- Never hardcode credentials or secrets.
- Store sensitive data in secure storage/keychain equivalents.
- Minimize permissions to what the feature actually needs.
- Add clear copy for data collection and camera/location usage.

## Testing and release checklist

- Unit tests for core business logic.
- Integration tests for critical flows (auth, payment, upload, checkout).
- Device coverage: small phone, large phone, and tablet (if supported).
- Verify deep links, push notification routes, and back-stack behavior.
- Verify dark mode, accessibility labels, and keyboard/voice support where applicable.

## Design source options

- For style anchoring, you may use a `DESIGN.md` file from `awesome-design-md`.
- For fast UI system recommendations, use `ui-ux-pro-max-skill` design system patterns.
- Keep brand adaptation explicit: cite chosen style family and why.

## Google Play Console Standards

When creating a new app in the Google Play Console, adhere to these standard baseline configurations for our game suite:

- **App name:** Must exactly match the canonical `displayName` (max 30 characters).
- **Package name:** Must match the reverse-DNS ID defined in the project (e.g., `com.bharatbrawlers.<slug>`). (max 150 characters).
- **Default language:** `English (United States) – en-US`.
- **App or game:** Select `Game` (unless explicitly building a non-game utility).
- **Free or paid:** Select `Free` (unless specifically building a premium SKU).
- **Declarations:** 
  - Check *Confirm app meets Developer Program Policies*.
  - Check *Accept US export laws*.

Always ensure these are recorded correctly upon creation as some (like Package name) are permanent.

### Creating a Production Release

When creating a production release or rolling out a new app bundle (.aab), use the following standard configurations:

- **Play Games Sidekick:** Leave **unchecked** (unless we are explicitly integrating Google Play Games Services achievements/leaderboards).
- **Release name:** Use the semantic version number from `build.gradle.kts` (e.g., `1.0.0`). If the Play Console auto-fills this from the AAB, leave it as is.
- **Release notes:** Must always be wrapped in the locale tags (e.g., `<en-US>...</en-US>`). Keep notes punchy, using bullet points to highlight new features or bug fixes. Example for V1:
  ```text
  <en-US>
  Initial release! 
  - Full game features
  - Fully playable offline
  </en-US>
  ```

### Privacy Policy

- **Privacy Policy URL:** When submitting an app to the Play Console (under App Content -> Privacy Policy), always use `https://dsapatterns.io/privacy.html`. This is a unified zero-data privacy policy covering all games in the suite.

### Content Ratings & App Content Declarations

When filling out the **App Content** questionnaires in the Google Play Console, use these standard zero-data/safe answers:

- **Content Ratings:**
  - Answer **No** to all sensitive topics (Violence, Fear, Sexuality, Gambling, Language, Substance, Crude Humor).
  - This ensures an E (Everyone) or PEGI 3 rating, keeping the app safe for all audiences.
- **Target Audience:**
  - Select **13-15**, **16-17**, and **18 and over**.
  - Appeal to children: **No**.
  - *(Reasoning: Selecting under 13 requires joining the Designed for Families program, which has stricter ad SDK and data compliance reviews. 13+ is the safest path for rapid approval).*
- **Data Safety:** 
  - Does your app collect or share any of the required user data types? **No**.
  - *(Our games are fully offline and use no analytics or crashlytics tracking by default).*
- **Government apps:** **No**.
- **Financial features:** **No**.
- **Health apps:** **No**.

